## 1. Product Requirements

### 1.1 Vision

Ekklesphere gives a church one place to know its people, care for them and run its operations. Its signature feature is attendance that takes care of itself: members who opt in are recognised when they arrive at the church premises, and members who stop showing up are surfaced to pastoral teams early, so no one drifts away unnoticed.

### 1.2 Problems to solve

- Paper registers and headcounts are slow, inaccurate and hard to analyse.
- Leaders learn that someone has drifted away months after it happened.
- Member data lives in spreadsheets, notebooks and WhatsApp groups.
- Most church software is priced and designed for Western markets, with weak support for mobile money, patchy connectivity and low-end Android phones.

### 1.3 Goals and non-goals

| Goals (v1) | Non-goals (v1) |
|---|---|
| Accurate, low-friction attendance for services and events | Live streaming or sermon hosting |
| One member database with households, groups and ministries | Full accounting, payroll or fund accounting |
| Timely follow-up of absent members and first-time visitors | Worship presentation or song software |
| Push, SMS and email communication | Any tracking of location away from church premises |
| Giving with mobile money and cards | A public social network |
| Works offline and on low-end Android | Support for non-church organisations |

### 1.4 Users

| Persona | Needs |
|---|---|
| Member | Check in effortlessly, give, see events, join groups, update own details |
| First-time visitor | Register in under a minute without installing anything |
| Parent or guardian | Check children in and out safely; control child data |
| Usher or greeter | Scan or search to check people in; see who is new |
| Group or cell leader | See own group roster, attendance and absentees |
| Pastor or care team | Follow-up list, member history, notes (restricted) |
| Church admin | Manage members, services, communications, reports |
| Finance officer | Record and reconcile giving; no access to pastoral notes |
| Platform owner | Manage churches (tenants), billing, support |

### 1.5 Core user stories

- As a member, I opt in once and am marked present when I arrive for service, with a notification confirming it.
- As a member without a smartphone, I get checked in by an usher using my phone number or a QR card.
- As a member who denied location access, I receive an email or SMS before service with a personal check-in link.
- As a pastor, I see everyone absent for three consecutive weeks and can assign a follow-up call.
- As an admin, I see attendance by service, campus, group and trend without building a spreadsheet.
- As a parent, I check my child in, and only a verified guardian can collect them.
- As a member, I can view, export and delete the data the church holds about me, and withdraw location consent at any time.

### 1.6 Success metrics

| Metric | Target |
|---|---|
| Auto check-in agreement with usher headcount audit (pilot) | 90% or better |
| Active members opted in to auto check-in within 3 months | 60% or better |
| Time to produce the weekly attendance report | Under 1 minute |
| Absent members contacted within 7 days of triggering follow-up | 80% or better |
| Battery use attributable to the app | Under 2% per day |
| Crash-free sessions | 99.5% or better |

### 1.7 Assumptions and constraints

- Android first (dominant in Ghana), iOS second. Connectivity is intermittent and data is costly, so the app must be small and offline tolerant.
- Phone number is the primary identity. Many members have no email address.
- Pilot with one church and one campus, but multi-tenant and multi-campus from day one.
- Legal: Ghana's Data Protection Act, 2012 (Act 843) applies. To my understanding it classes religious belief as special personal data, which makes church membership data sensitive. Confirm obligations, including registration with the Data Protection Commission, with a Ghanaian lawyer before launch.

