## 2. System Architecture

### 2.1 Overview

```
Member app (React Native) | Admin web (Next.js) | Usher PWA
  geofence, BLE, push     | dashboards, CRUD     | QR scan, search
            |                      |                   |
            +------- HTTPS / JSON API (REST + webhooks) -------+
                                   |
                     API gateway / load balancer
                                   |
            Backend modular monolith (NestJS, TypeScript)
  Auth | Members | Services | Attendance | Comms | Giving | Groups | Reports
        |                    |                     |
  PostgreSQL + PostGIS   Redis (cache, queues)   Object storage
        |
  Background workers (BullMQ): attendance resolver, notifications, reports
        |
  External: FCM/APNs | Email (SES/Postmark) | SMS (Hubtel/Arkesel) | Paystack
```

### 2.2 Technology choices

| Layer | Choice | Why |
|---|---|---|
| Mobile | React Native with Expo development builds | One codebase for Android and iOS; native modules for geofencing and BLE |
| Admin web | Next.js with TypeScript | Fast dashboards, shares types with the backend |
| Usher tool | Installable PWA | No app install for volunteers; works offline |
| Backend | NestJS (TypeScript) modular monolith | Simple to run and hire for; modules can be split later |
| Database | PostgreSQL 16 with PostGIS | Relational data, row-level security, geospatial queries |
| Cache and queues | Redis with BullMQ | Reliable background jobs, rate limiting |
| Files | S3-compatible object storage | Profile photos, exports, backups |
| Push | Firebase Cloud Messaging and APNs | Standard and free |
| Email | Amazon SES or Postmark | Deliverability, templates, bounce handling |
| SMS | Hubtel or Arkesel (local), Twilio fallback | Better rates and delivery in Ghana |
| Payments | Paystack or Hubtel | MTN MoMo, Telecel Cash, AirtelTigo Money and cards |
| Hosting | AWS af-south-1 (Cape Town) or equivalent, containers | Closest major region to Accra |
| Observability | Sentry, OpenTelemetry, Grafana | Crash and performance visibility |

### 2.3 Attendance pipeline

1. The member opts in; the app registers geofences for the campus (inner and outer rings) with the operating system.
2. The OS wakes the app on entering the outer ring (about 300 m). The app records an approach signal and, inside a service window, schedules a dwell check.
3. On entering the inner ring (about 100 m), or on detecting the entrance BLE beacon, the app sends a signed signal to the API, queued locally if offline.
4. The attendance resolver matches the signal to the open service instance, applies the rules (window, dwell, duplicates) and writes one attendance record with a method and a confidence score.
5. The member gets a push confirmation. If push is unavailable, an SMS or email is sent.
6. A nightly job and the live dashboard flag members absent for N consecutive services and create follow-up tasks.

The device sends only an arrival event for a campus. Raw location history is never uploaded.

### 2.4 Multi-tenancy

- The tenant is the church organisation; each tenant has one or more campuses.
- Every tenant-owned table carries tenant_id, enforced with PostgreSQL row-level security as well as application checks.
- Tenant-level settings: branding, service times, geofence radii, retention periods, consent text.

### 2.5 Offline and sync

- The mobile app and the usher PWA store pending check-ins in a local queue with a client-generated UUID, so retries are idempotent.
- The usher PWA caches the day's member list and syncs on reconnect.
- The server accepts events with an original timestamp up to 24 hours old and validates them against the service window.

### 2.6 Environments

Local (Docker Compose), staging and production. Infrastructure as code (Terraform), CI/CD with GitHub Actions, automated database migrations, daily encrypted backups with tested restores.

