## 4. Security and Privacy

### 4.1 Principles

Consent first, data minimisation, least privilege, defence in depth and full auditability. Church membership reveals religious belief, so this system is treated as holding sensitive data from day one.

### 4.2 Location privacy by design (mandatory)

- Opt-in is separate from account creation, in plain language, and can be set per campus.
- Geofencing is performed by the operating system on the device. The app uploads only an arrival or departure event tied to a campus and service, never a location trail.
- Raw signals are deleted after 90 days; attendance records follow the church's retention policy.
- Withdrawing consent takes one tap, stops signals immediately and is enforced on the server too.
- Location data is never used for advertising, analytics or sharing with third parties.
- Children's devices are never geofenced.

### 4.3 Authentication and sessions

- Phone OTP: 6 digits, 5-minute expiry, rate limited per number, device and IP; a new device triggers re-verification (a guard against SIM-swap abuse).
- Email magic links are single use and expire in 15 minutes.
- Access tokens last 15 minutes; refresh tokens rotate and are bound to the device, with server-side revocation.
- Admin, pastor and finance roles require multi-factor authentication (TOTP or passkeys).
- Lockouts and brute-force protection on every credential endpoint.

### 4.4 Authorisation (role-based)

| Capability | Member | Usher | Group leader | Pastor | Finance | Admin |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| View and edit own profile | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes | Yes |
| Check others in | No | Yes | Own group | No | No | Yes |
| View member contact details | No | Limited | Own group | Yes | Limited | Yes |
| View attendance reports | No | Current service | Own group | Yes | No | Yes |
| Pastoral notes | No | No | No | Yes | No | No |
| View giving | Own | No | No | Totals only | Yes | Totals only |
| Send communications | No | No | Own group | Yes | No | Yes |
| Manage roles and settings | No | No | No | No | No | Yes |

### 4.5 Data protection

- TLS 1.2 or higher everywhere with HSTS; encryption at rest for the database, backups and object storage.
- Field-level encryption for the most sensitive fields (pastoral notes, any national ID), with keys in a managed key service.
- Secrets in a managed secrets store; none are embedded in the app binary.
- Tenant isolation through row-level security, with isolation tests in CI.
- Backups are encrypted and restores are tested every quarter.

### 4.6 Application and mobile security

- Build to OWASP ASVS level 2 and OWASP MASVS: input validation, parameterised queries, rate limits, strict CSP.
- Mobile: Play Integrity and App Attest to confirm a genuine app, Keychain and Keystore for storage, certificate pinning with a rotation plan, and no sensitive data in logs.
- Arrival payloads are signed by the device; posted QR codes rotate every 30 to 60 seconds.
- Dependency, static-analysis and secret scanning in CI; an independent penetration test before launch and yearly after.
- Payments: verify webhook signatures, use idempotency keys and never store card data (Paystack holds that scope).

### 4.7 Compliance and member rights

- Store a consent record for every grant or withdrawal: what, when, which text version, which channel.
- Data subject rights (access, export, correction, deletion, objection) are self-service in the app and handled by admins within 30 days.
- Before the pilot: register with the Data Protection Commission, name a data protection supervisor, complete a data protection impact assessment and write a breach response plan.
- Keep an immutable audit log of who viewed or changed what, retained for two years.
- Take legal advice on hosting data outside Ghana, since cross-border transfer rules apply.

