## 9. Implementation Plan

### 9.1 Team

One tech lead or architect, two full-stack TypeScript engineers, one React Native engineer, a part-time designer, a part-time QA engineer and part-time DevOps support. A product owner from the pilot church should be available weekly.

### 9.2 Phases

| Phase | Weeks | Scope | Exit criteria |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0. Discovery and foundations | 1 to 3 | Confirm name; legal advice and Data Protection Commission plan; data protection impact assessment; choose pilot church; interview ushers, pastors and members; geofence field spike on target phones at the real premises; design tokens; infrastructure, CI/CD and environments | Signed-off scope, legal path clear, spike shows geofence behaviour on target devices |
| 1. Core platform | 4 to 11 | Tenants, auth (phone OTP and magic link), roles, member and household import, services and events, usher PWA, QR and manual attendance, admin dashboard, basic push, SMS and email, audit log | The pilot church runs a full Sunday digitally with usher and QR attendance |
| 2. Proximity attendance | 12 to 17 | Mobile geofencing with dwell, beacons, consent flows, email and SMS check-in links, attendance resolver, spoofing checks, manufacturer battery guides; run in shadow mode for 4 Sundays against usher counts | 90% agreement with headcount audit; battery use under 2% per day; opt-in flow tested with real members |
| 3. Care and communication | 18 to 22 | Absence rules, follow-up tasks, visitor pipeline, templates and segments, groups and rosters, group attendance | Pastoral team works entirely from the follow-up list for 3 weeks |
| 4. Giving and children | 23 to 28 | Mobile money and card giving, funds, receipts and statements, children's check-in and secure pickup | Reconciled test donations; child check-in dry run passes |
| 5. Hardening and launch | 29 to 32 | Penetration test and fixes, load test at 20 times peak, Data Protection Commission registration complete, store submissions (background location declarations), staff training, onboard two or three more churches | Launch checklist complete, no open critical or high security findings |

Timelines assume the team above and are estimates; the Phase 0 field spike and store review times are the most likely sources of change.

### 9.3 Risks and mitigations

| Risk | Likelihood | Mitigation |
|---|---|---|
| Manufacturer battery managers stop background geofences | High | In-app setup guides, silence detection, beacon and QR fallbacks, test on Tecno, Infinix and itel early |
| GPS inaccuracy in dense urban areas | Medium | Dwell rule, beacons at entrances, tune radii from field tests, shadow-mode calibration |
| Members reluctant to share location | Medium | Clear consent copy, the privacy centre, full functionality without it, visible leadership endorsement |
| Legal or regulatory gaps | Medium | Early legal advice, DPIA, Commission registration before pilot, data minimisation by design |
| Google Play or App Store rejects background location | Medium | Prepare declaration and demo video early, show a clear user-facing benefit, keep beacon and QR paths as a fallback |
| SMS cost overrun | Medium | Prefer push and email, per-tenant spending caps, batch reminders |
| Poor connectivity on Sunday | High | Offline queues in app and PWA, idempotent sync |
| Scope creep | High | Hold P1 and P2 modules behind phase gates; pilot feedback decides order |
| Data breach | Low to medium | Encryption, row-level security, MFA, audit log, penetration test, breach response plan |

### 9.4 Decisions to make now

1. Confirm the name Ekklesphere after domain, trademark and app store checks.
2. Choose the pilot church and a named product owner there.
3. Choose the pricing model (for example per active member per month in cedis, with a free tier for small churches).
4. Confirm cross-platform React Native against fully native Android and iOS after the Phase 0 geofence spike.
5. Decide whether to buy BLE beacons for the pilot entrance (inexpensive, and they greatly raise confidence).
6. Pick the hosting region and confirm cross-border transfer advice with counsel.

### 9.5 Suggested first two weeks

- Week 1: book the legal consult, pick the pilot church, register domains and handles, set up the repository, CI and staging, and write the geofence spike app.
- Week 2: walk and drive the premises with 6 to 8 different phones logging enter and exit events, interview ushers and the pastoral team, draft the consent screen and run it past a few members, and finalise Phase 1 scope.
